@Programmer → T020: [WHAT] Send agent_statuses immediately on WebSocket connect
[CONTEXT] Clients connecting don't receive current agent statuses, UI shows nothing until next status change
[CONSTRAINTS] In websocket_endpoint(), send current agent_statuses dict right after accept(). Use existing message format.

@Programmer → T021: [WHAT] Add WebSocket broadcast for agent stats cards
[CONTEXT] Agent stats cards only update on HTTP fetch at page load, not real-time
[CONSTRAINTS] Broadcast agent stats when they change, or add polling interval. Use existing broadcast_tasks pattern.

@Programmer → T022: [WHAT] Reduce MIN_DISPLAY_MS delay from 2000ms
[CONTEXT] Activity bar has artificial 2s delay making status feel laggy
[CONSTRAINTS] Reduce to 500ms or remove. In activity bar update logic in studio.html.

@Programmer → T023: [WHAT] Split Hub tab into 60/40 layout - messages left, tasks panel right
[CONTEXT] Hub messages only use ~50% width, wasted space. User wants to see tasks alongside hub activity.
[CONSTRAINTS] Use CSS flexbox/grid. Tasks panel shows current task list with status. Maintain responsive behavior.

@Programmer → T024: [WHAT] Fix Hub 60/40 split - remove duplicate .chat-area rule at line 399 that overrides flex:6 with flex:1
[CONTEXT] Layout showing 20/80 instead of 60/40 due to CSS conflict
[CONSTRAINTS] Keep line 250's flex:6, delete or merge line 399's rule

@Programmer → T025: [WHAT] Change Hub layout to 80/20 split - update .chat-area flex:6→8 and .hub-tasks-sidebar flex:4→2
[CONTEXT] User wants more space for Hub messages
[CONSTRAINTS] Edit lines 251 and 259 in studio.html

@Designer → T026: [WHAT] Audit hub-tasks-sidebar features and document what must be preserved in task detail window
[CONTEXT] Removing sidebar, porting features to task detail popup
[CONSTRAINTS] List: action buttons, status display, agent badge, description - confirm nothing lost

@Programmer → T027: [WHAT] Remove .hub-tasks-sidebar from Hub, ensure task detail window has: full description, agent badge, status, Cancel/Retry/Delete buttons, token stats
[CONTEXT] Per T026 audit - consolidating task UI
[CONSTRAINTS] Don't break existing task detail functionality, keep all action buttons working

@QA → T028: [WHAT] Test task detail window after sidebar removal - verify all actions work
[CONTEXT] UI consolidation
[CONSTRAINTS] Test Cancel, Retry, Delete, token display

@Taxonomy → T036: [WHAT] Analyze hub_history.json and tasks_archive.json to extract and categorize all friction points, errors, misunderstandings, and lessons learned. Produce a taxonomy of error types and patterns.
[CONTEXT] We have raw data scattered across chat logs and task archives. Need to understand what categories of friction exist before deciding on storage structure.
[CONSTRAINTS] Focus on actual incidents from the data - tool syntax issues, naming errors (Qa vs QA), timeouts, prompt misunderstandings. Output a structured analysis with categories and specific examples from the data.

@Research → T037: [WHAT] Analyze the Schedules/Cron tab UX and recommend how to organize it for maximum CEO insight on automated workflows. Compare current heartbeat creation flow vs task-native approach (CEO creates tasks normally, marks as recurring). Include wireframe concepts.
[CONTEXT] CEO finds heartbeat creation tedious - automated task chains are intricate. Need to leverage existing task-writing mental model.
[CONSTRAINTS] Must integrate with current task format (assignee, dependencies, WHAT/CONTEXT/CONSTRAINTS). Consider: visibility of next runs, chain dependencies, pause/resume, history.

@Programmer → T038: [WHAT] Extend Task dataclass with routine fields: is_routine_template, routine_interval_seconds, chain_group, run_number, next_run, routine_source_id. Update to_dict/from_dict serialization.
[CONTEXT] Research report T037 recommends task-native approach where routines are just tasks with scheduling metadata.
[CONSTRAINTS] Keep backwards compatibility with existing tasks. Add fields as Optional with sensible defaults.

@Programmer → T039: [WHAT] Rename Heartbeats tab to 'Routines' in studio.html. Redesign UI per Research wireframes: status pills (ACTIVE/PAUSED/RUNNING), timeline bar (next/last run), chain progress indicators (✓ ● ○), run counter, quick action buttons (Pause/Resume/Run Now/Delete).
[CONTEXT] CEO finds current heartbeat UI tedious. New design emphasizes visibility and matches task-native mental model.
[CONSTRAINTS] Rename all CSS classes heartbeat-* to routine-*. Update all JS functions. Preserve existing functionality while improving layout.

@Programmer → T040: [WHAT] Update 'Create Routine' modal to use task-native approach: chain builder where each task uses standard WHAT/CONTEXT/CONSTRAINTS format. Add interval selector, dependency visualization.
[CONTEXT] CEO writes tasks normally, marks them as recurring. Same mental model, just adds scheduling metadata.
[CONSTRAINTS] Each chain step should feel like creating a regular task. Dependencies inferred as linear by default.

@Programmer → T041: [WHAT] Rename all remaining 'heartbeat' references to 'schedule' throughout the codebase: WebSocket message types (create_heartbeat → create_schedule), backend functions, API endpoints, data structures, and any comments/docs.
[CONTEXT] Terminology cleanup. 'Routines' tab is done (T039), but backend still uses heartbeat terminology. 'Schedule' is clearer than heartbeat for recurring task execution.
[CONSTRAINTS] Must update: server.py, any heartbeat-related Python files, WebSocket message handlers in studio.html. Ensure frontend JS calls match new backend message types.

@Taxonomy → T042: [WHAT] Audit all Python, JavaScript, CSS, and HTML code in the studio codebase. Review naming conventions, code organization, file structure, and terminology consistency. Produce a report documenting findings.
[CONTEXT] Post-refactor audit after heartbeat→schedule/routine rename. Need expert review of current state before further development.
[CONSTRAINTS] Read-only audit. NO code changes. Output findings as a structured report covering: naming inconsistencies, dead code, organizational issues, style violations.
